<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vagharshapat</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vagharshapat is a city in the Armavir province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yerevan">Yerevan</a>. Founded or renamed by the Arsacid king Vagharsh I in the 2nd century CE, it became the capital of Armenia after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxata">Artaxata</a>, and it is the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etchmiadzin-cathedral">Etchmiadzin Cathedral</a>, seat of the Catholicos of the Armenian Apostolic Church. The city was officially called Etchmiadzin for much of the 20th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vagharshapat.jpg" alt="Vagharshapat" width="320" height="217" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Areg Amirkhanian · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D4%B7%D5%BB%D5%B4%D5%AB%D5%A1%D5%AE%D5%B6%D5%AB_%D5%84%D5%A1%D5%B5%D6%80_%D5%8F%D5%A1%D5%B3%D5%A1%D6%80.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Vagharshapat lies about 18 kilometres west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yerevan">Yerevan</a> in the basin of the Kasagh River, in the north-eastern end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-ararat">Ararat</a> plain, at about 853 metres above sea level. The area was inhabited from the 3rd millennium BCE. The first written record is in the inscriptions of the Urartian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rusa-ii">Rusa II</a>, who opened a water canal there, and the most probable site of the ancient city is Shresh Hill near the Kasagh River. According to the 5th-century writer Movses Khorenatsi, the place was first called Artimed and was later rebuilt as Vardgesavan during the reign of King Orontes I.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the first half of the 2nd century CE the Arsacid king Vagharsh I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> renovated the old town of Vardgesavan and renamed it Vagharshapat. It was the capital of the Arsacid Kingdom of Armenia from 120 to 330. After Armenia adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> as a state religion in 301, the city became the seat of the Armenian Catholicosate and was gradually called Ejmiatsin, after its Mother Cathedral. The name means the descent of the Only-Begotten, for a vision of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-the-illuminator">Gregory the Illuminator</a> had on the spot. The political capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dvin">Dvin</a> in 336, and the seat of the Catholicosate followed in 452.</p><p>Vagharshapat remained the country's most important city until the fall of the Arsacid Kingdom in 428, and the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> library in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> was founded there in 480. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> conquered it in 658. It revived briefly under the Bagratid Kingdom, was overrun by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> in 1045 and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> in 1064, and became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> in the 13th century. From 1502 to 1828 it was under Persian rule, with short periods of Ottoman rule, and it passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-turkmenchay">Treaty of Turkmenchay</a> on 21 February 1828. In 1833 two visitors described it as a village of about 500 mud cabins.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Vagharshapat is the fifth largest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and a suburb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yerevan">Yerevan</a> in effect. It was officially named Echmiatsin from 1945 to 1995 and is still commonly called Ejmiatsin. It is the seat of the Armenian Apostolic Church and is known as Armenia's spiritual capital. In 2000 the churches of Vagharshapat and the ruins of Zvartnots Cathedral were designated a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. Etchmiadzin Cathedral was built in 301 to 303 and enlarged in 480, 618 and 1658, and the Churches of Saint Hripsime, built in 618, and Saint Gayane, built in 630, also stand there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vagharshapat” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Artashat kept its importance until it lost its status as capital, and the kingdom's cities, <b>Vagharshapat</b>, later Ejmiatsin, and Dvin, built by Khosrov III in 335, carried Armenian statehood on under the Arsacids.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-armenia-artaxiad">Kingdom of Armenia (Artaxiad) · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Kings ruled from Artaxata and later <b>Vagharshapat</b> over powerful hereditary noble houses such as the Mamikonians and Bagratunis.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-armenia-arsacid">Kingdom of Armenia (Arsacid)</a></li></ul></section>
